  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Function
    
    Memoria∴moria is the Nightshelf memorial-law that transforms loss into architectural permanence. No disaster is administratively real until it is carved here; no death is official until the Fire Keepers authorize its inscription in the ice-walls of Nightfall Shelf, the labyrinthine colony carved into the Berg's shadowed interior. The rule binds survival to refusal to forget — and, more consequentially, grants the Fire Keepers authority over *what* is remembered and *how*.
    
    ## Origin and Growth
    
    The memorial began as a single monument to Augur ☽'Silvsiaelrous~ the Resolute, a crystalline-weaver and orca negotiator of the ☽'shadow Covens' Order who died during the Abyss Collapse Tide: a fire-core hammer with a cracked head, mounted in ice that never melts, in a chamber the aurora's light cannot reach. Successive disasters enlarged it. The Vum∴tenebra added names beside the Augur's. The Rofundis∴rupt, which collapsed entire tunnel-systems, and the Profun∴lapsum — forty-two names from that disaster alone, though I note the usual caveats about round-adjacent casualty figures — expanded Memoria∴moria into walls of names, weapon-racks holding relics no one uses, and memorial-stones that glow with captured fire so the dead can be seen in darkness. The Bra∴conflictu, the Hisper∴vi, the Mbra∴silentiu, the Ofundis∴flux, the Thron∴mbra Fracta, the Pta∴extinct: each carved in turn. The Faction Wars produced most of these. The memorial is, in effect, a map of that era's cost.
    
    ## Contested Inscriptions
    
    Fire Keepers have sent revised instructions to the Remembrancers, amending *missing* to *confirmed lost* for those whose bodies were never recovered. The Shadow Parliament has pressed for faction wars to be carved as inevitable rather than chosen. The Remembrancer Obscureth~, whose account survives in chronicle, recorded the tension directly: she carved the official narrative the Fire Keepers demanded for Silvsiaelrous~ while encoding the more complex truth through the precision of her craft — what she chose to include, and what she left as knowing silence.
    
    ## The Remembrancers
    
    The guild answers only to the deepest vaults. They work three levels below the memorial-stones by rationed fire-core lamplight, using tools reserved for each inscription element: fine-points for names, broad chisels for weapons, curved blades for dates in the old script.
    
    They have begun to whisper that the memorial grows faster than new tragedies arrive. That some names appear before the deaths they commemorate.
    
    No one has asked them directly.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
